# The LLVM/MLIR revision this project builds and links against.
#
# One git tag from llvm/llvm-project, and nothing else. No other file in the
# repository names an LLVM revision; every artifact and every consumer derives
# its name from this line.
#
# What reads it:
#
#   .github/workflows/toolchain.yml
#       Builds LLVM/MLIR at this tag and publishes the installed prefix twice:
#       as container images tagged <flavour>-<arch>-<this line>, and as tarballs
#       attached to a GitHub prerelease tagged toolchain-<this line>.
#
#   .github/workflows/release.yml
#       Pulls the image whose tag carries this line, and copies the prefix out
#       of it into the release build image.
#
#   .github/actions/restore-llvm-toolchain
#       Downloads the tarball from the release whose tag carries this line, and
#       unpacks it into the CI container.
#
# Changing this line changes what is built and what everything is named.
#
# It is not the only thing that triggers a rebuild: the toolchain is rebuilt
# whenever its build configuration changes, which is this line plus the CMake
# flags in build_llvm.py and the Dockerfiles. A change to any of those produces
# a different toolchain under the same name, so the Toolchain workflow compares
# a hash of all three against what it published rather than trusting the tag.
#
# Nothing silently falls back to a toolchain built from some other revision:
# each consumer derives the name it needs from this file and fails loudly if
# that artifact has not been published yet.
#
# The revision is a semantic input to the generated code rather than a build
# detail. The C backend is routed through MLIR/EmitC, whose printed output can
# vary across MLIR versions, so which LLVM is linked is part of what the
# compiler emits. See docs/reference/guarantees/supply-chain.md.
llvmorg-22.1.8
